WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.317 --> 00:00:03.213
- But I got up and he
said, "Now, is it empty?

00:00:03.213 --> 00:00:04.212
"Is it empty?"

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:06.429
And I got up and I screamed, "No!"

00:00:06.429 --> 00:00:09.106
I'm sort of on a tirade
trying to promote this film,

00:00:09.106 --> 00:00:11.422
'cause I just want to see more
films like this out there,

00:00:11.422 --> 00:00:12.556
as a fan of movies.

00:00:12.556 --> 00:00:14.509
It was the first memory that I had.

00:00:14.509 --> 00:00:16.653
It's the first thing I can think of

00:00:16.653 --> 00:00:18.444
is saying, "I want to be an actor."

00:00:18.444 --> 00:00:20.340
"Don't you pull that
(bleeping) with me. (laughs)

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.187
He's like, "You get in that
God damn cockpit right now."

00:00:23.187 --> 00:00:24.781
When you find a great piece of material

00:00:24.781 --> 00:00:26.035
or a great script, it's like

00:00:26.035 --> 00:00:27.762
you should what happens.

00:00:27.762 --> 00:00:29.248
It's like a pirana feeding fest.

00:00:29.248 --> 00:00:32.270
I don't know where I got those
little balls as 16-years-old

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:35.270
to say, "No, I'm going to wait it out."

00:00:37.106 --> 00:00:39.697
I kind of felt like I'd
already died and gone to heaven

00:00:39.697 --> 00:00:40.927
'cause no one said anything.

00:00:40.927 --> 00:00:42.751
(audience laughing)

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:44.158
And I was screaming at the top of my lungs

00:00:44.158 --> 00:00:45.900
saying, "What the hell is going on here?"

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.584
- He's an American
actor and film producer.

00:00:48.584 --> 00:00:50.360
He achieved international fame

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.621
with James Cameron's epic
romance, Titanic, in 1997.

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:56.920
He's been nominated
for six Academy Awards.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.482
And, in 2016, won the
Academy Award for best actor

00:01:01.482 --> 00:01:03.143
for his movie, The Revenant.

00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:05.901
Here's Leonardo DiCaprio
and here's my take

00:01:05.901 --> 00:01:08.568
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:13.051
- Do you remember the audition
when you first met DeNiro?

00:01:13.051 --> 00:01:16.221
- That was, yeah, I
mean, that one of those

00:01:16.221 --> 00:01:18.427
incredible auditions that came around

00:01:18.427 --> 00:01:21.306
that I supposed every
kid had been waiting for

00:01:21.306 --> 00:01:22.734
for a long period of time,

00:01:22.734 --> 00:01:25.230
because it's just not that often

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.510
that you get a starring
role above Robert DeNiro

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:31.694
and this incredible screenplay,

00:01:31.694 --> 00:01:33.887
and with Ellen Barkin
and Michael Caton-Jones.

00:01:33.887 --> 00:01:34.720
I mean, it was just...

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.978
Everyone was sort of
feending for this role.

00:01:36.978 --> 00:01:40.753
And I got down, I suppose,
to the final five.

00:01:40.753 --> 00:01:42.847
And it was like a last sort of day.

00:01:42.847 --> 00:01:47.014
Toby was there and I do
remember thinking to myself,

00:01:48.094 --> 00:01:49.761
"I got to do something.

00:01:49.761 --> 00:01:51.392
"I got to do something to stand out."

00:01:51.392 --> 00:01:55.938
And in the audition it was Art
Linson, Michael Caton-Jones.

00:01:55.938 --> 00:01:58.996
Art Linson was the producer, and DeNiro.

00:01:58.996 --> 00:02:02.496
And I remember it was a mustard jar scene.

00:02:03.443 --> 00:02:07.599
And he had to jam a mustard
jar in my eye repeatedly.

00:02:07.599 --> 00:02:08.897
It was an abuse scene.

00:02:08.897 --> 00:02:12.034
And the script sort of didn't call for it,

00:02:12.034 --> 00:02:14.529
but I got up, he said, "Now, is it empty?

00:02:14.529 --> 00:02:15.362
"Is it empty?"

00:02:15.362 --> 00:02:18.144
And I got up and I screamed, "No!"

00:02:18.144 --> 00:02:19.678
Like, completely unnecessary,

00:02:19.678 --> 00:02:21.521
but I screamed in his face.
(audience laughing)

00:02:21.521 --> 00:02:23.773
And I sat there, (laughs) you know,

00:02:23.773 --> 00:02:26.912
with my head looking like a red tomato

00:02:26.912 --> 00:02:30.254
and everyone started laughing at me

00:02:30.254 --> 00:02:31.821
in the entire room, DeNiro,

00:02:31.821 --> 00:02:33.692
everybody was in hysterics.

00:02:33.692 --> 00:02:35.273
And I sat there frozen just thinking,

00:02:35.273 --> 00:02:37.800
"Oh, my God, I just screwed this entire

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.753
"opportunity up."
(audience laughing)

00:02:39.753 --> 00:02:41.353
And I just sat there frozen.

00:02:41.353 --> 00:02:45.128
And then, Bob, in
traditional DeNiro fashion

00:02:45.128 --> 00:02:46.997
kind of just looked at me and went,

00:02:46.997 --> 00:02:47.830
"It's good, was good."

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.830
(audience laughing)

00:02:54.036 --> 00:02:56.324
And then we carried on doing the scene.

00:02:56.324 --> 00:03:00.404
And I actually had dinner
with DeNiro recently

00:03:00.404 --> 00:03:02.901
and he told me, "You know,
I was the one who said,

00:03:02.901 --> 00:03:05.977
"I was the one who said
you should get the job."

00:03:05.977 --> 00:03:10.006
I'm very proud that a
movie like this got made.

00:03:10.006 --> 00:03:13.624
It's very, very, very rare to see films

00:03:13.624 --> 00:03:14.951
that are this ambitious

00:03:14.951 --> 00:03:17.658
coming out of the Hollywood studio system.

00:03:17.658 --> 00:03:21.097
So I'm sort of on a tirade
trying to promote this film,

00:03:21.097 --> 00:03:23.578
'cause I just want to see more
films like this out there,

00:03:23.578 --> 00:03:25.148
as a fan of movies.

00:03:25.148 --> 00:03:26.891
But as far as awards are concerned,

00:03:26.891 --> 00:03:29.418
you know, the truth is there's a lot of

00:03:29.418 --> 00:03:31.640
peace of mind in it,
because you really realize

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.740
that you ultimately have no
control over these things.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:39.334
- Is it something you hope
for or set off to the side.

00:03:39.334 --> 00:03:41.381
- Like I said, you know, honestly,

00:03:41.381 --> 00:03:45.337
I do these movies because
I really love movies,

00:03:45.337 --> 00:03:46.541
and I want to make great movies.

00:03:46.541 --> 00:03:50.794
All that stuff is totally beyond
your control, it really is.

00:03:50.794 --> 00:03:54.366
Well, I do these Jack Nicholson eye.

00:03:54.366 --> 00:03:57.699
(interviewers laughing)

00:04:03.329 --> 00:04:05.599
- [Interviewer] Thank you.

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:07.984
- Has acting turned out to be
all that you wanted it to be?

00:04:07.984 --> 00:04:10.901
- It's incredible. It's incredible.

00:04:13.313 --> 00:04:16.980
I never knew that I
could do it as a career.

00:04:18.271 --> 00:04:20.112
I never knew...

00:04:20.112 --> 00:04:21.661
It was the first memory that had.

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:24.304
It's the first thing I
can thing of is saying,

00:04:24.304 --> 00:04:25.615
"I want to be an actor."

00:04:25.615 --> 00:04:26.673
to my parents.

00:04:26.673 --> 00:04:29.266
And if I hadn't lived in Los Angeles

00:04:29.266 --> 00:04:31.681
and been in such close proximity

00:04:31.681 --> 00:04:34.594
to sort of the mecca of movie making.

00:04:34.594 --> 00:04:36.975
If I didn't have parents that said,

00:04:36.975 --> 00:04:38.461
"Okay, we'll drive you to auditions."

00:04:38.461 --> 00:04:40.237
We didn't have the economic means

00:04:40.237 --> 00:04:43.163
to move camp from Idaho to Los Angeles

00:04:43.163 --> 00:04:45.503
because some snotty 10-year-old kid says,

00:04:45.503 --> 00:04:46.505
"I want to be an actor!"

00:04:46.505 --> 00:04:48.129
(host laughing)

00:04:48.129 --> 00:04:49.810
We did it, and after school
I got to go on auditions.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:52.274
But it always, and I've said this before,

00:04:52.274 --> 00:04:54.158
it felt like an elite group of people

00:04:54.158 --> 00:04:56.804
and it felt like I would
never belong to them.

00:04:56.804 --> 00:04:59.370
So once I got the opportunity to,

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:02.063
for example, get the movie This Boy's Life

00:05:02.063 --> 00:05:03.222
with Robert DeNiro.

00:05:03.222 --> 00:05:05.663
I said, wow, now I'm able to...

00:05:05.663 --> 00:05:08.901
I might be able to steer
the course of my career.

00:05:08.901 --> 00:05:11.619
I might be able to have a
career similar to these guys

00:05:11.619 --> 00:05:13.036
that I so admire.

00:05:14.229 --> 00:05:16.948
And that, I knew at 15-years-old,

00:05:16.948 --> 00:05:18.918
was the biggest gift in the world.

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:21.046
And that's never left me.

00:05:21.046 --> 00:05:23.322
I've never, I've never,

00:05:23.322 --> 00:05:27.126
that thirst or that
hunger for wanting to give

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:29.366
one great performance that
I'm truly satisfied with,

00:05:29.366 --> 00:05:32.180
or be in one movie that I
said, "I love this film."

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.101
Is something that I don't
know will ever be quenched.

00:05:36.101 --> 00:05:37.102
I don't know.

00:05:37.102 --> 00:05:39.237
And I think that's a good thing,

00:05:39.237 --> 00:05:41.747
because it keeps pushing me, you know?

00:05:41.747 --> 00:05:44.785
Constantly dissatisfied, in other words.

00:05:44.785 --> 00:05:46.994
I mean, I never went to a formal acting...

00:05:46.994 --> 00:05:50.222
It was only until really The Aviator

00:05:50.222 --> 00:05:53.043
that I started working with Larry Moss.

00:05:53.043 --> 00:05:56.210
It was the first film that I produced,

00:05:59.237 --> 00:06:01.762
that I had thought of, it
was sort of my concept.

00:06:01.762 --> 00:06:05.929
And I felt like I needed to
brush up on my technique.

00:06:08.179 --> 00:06:10.609
So that's when I really
started doing formal training.

00:06:10.609 --> 00:06:14.773
And it was an amazing experience with him.

00:06:14.773 --> 00:06:18.940
I mean, it was more than learning
how to be a better actor.

00:06:20.102 --> 00:06:21.334
He was a life coach.

00:06:21.334 --> 00:06:23.890
It was like (laughs),
he's like, the first day,

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:25.968
he was like, "All right, you
want to go over this stuff?"

00:06:25.968 --> 00:06:27.570
I'm, like, "Yeah, yeah, let's do it."

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:29.320
And he's like, "Okay,
you're Howard Hughes,

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:31.297
"you're in your plane,
this is your cocoon,

00:06:31.297 --> 00:06:32.502
"this is your sanctuary.

00:06:32.502 --> 00:06:34.513
"Show me how you fly a plane."

00:06:34.513 --> 00:06:36.100
I'm like, "Well, (laughs)
I haven't really."

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:37.353
He's like, "Come on.

00:06:37.353 --> 00:06:40.755
"Basically sit in that chair
and do it for me right now."

00:06:40.755 --> 00:06:42.601
I'm like, "Well, I
haven't quite prepared."

00:06:42.601 --> 00:06:44.550
He's like, "Don't you pull
that (bleeping) with me."

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.037
(laughs) He's like,

00:06:46.037 --> 00:06:47.860
"You get in that God
damn cockpit right now

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.736
"and you show me who the
hell Howard Hughes is.

00:06:49.736 --> 00:06:51.351
"You do it in front of me right now."

00:06:51.351 --> 00:06:52.552
I'm like, "Okay, (bleeping)."

00:06:52.552 --> 00:06:55.714
(audience laughing)

00:06:55.714 --> 00:06:58.802
I mean, and from there, we started working

00:06:58.802 --> 00:07:01.389
on breaking down the script

00:07:01.389 --> 00:07:03.305
in a way that I'd never had before.

00:07:03.305 --> 00:07:05.081
- I have to say I'm impressed,

00:07:05.081 --> 00:07:08.808
because most people don't
get an Oscar nomination

00:07:08.808 --> 00:07:11.403
and have many hit films behind them

00:07:11.403 --> 00:07:14.183
and then choose to go see an acting coach.

00:07:14.183 --> 00:07:18.350
- I think everyone can always
brush up on their technique.

00:07:19.372 --> 00:07:22.647
It is, you know, what we
do takes a lot of research.

00:07:22.647 --> 00:07:24.795
I mean, if you really
want to inhabit a role,

00:07:24.795 --> 00:07:26.862
it takes a lot of work and training

00:07:26.862 --> 00:07:29.660
and I think it's incredibly
important to constantly,

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:34.165
no matter how successful you
are, it's incredibly important.

00:07:34.165 --> 00:07:37.915
You know, what gives me
strength all the time

00:07:39.473 --> 00:07:42.966
is to be able to have formulated

00:07:42.966 --> 00:07:46.033
a group of people around
me that are my friends

00:07:46.033 --> 00:07:47.473
and my family.

00:07:47.473 --> 00:07:50.851
Those two words are meshed, in my opinion.

00:07:50.851 --> 00:07:53.768
But it's given me a grounding that,

00:07:55.936 --> 00:07:57.954
as insane as my life can be sometimes,

00:07:57.954 --> 00:08:00.569
is always refreshing and they're always

00:08:00.569 --> 00:08:02.914
so honest with me, and
I'm so thankful for that.

00:08:02.914 --> 00:08:04.156
Everything, at the end of the day,

00:08:04.156 --> 00:08:05.155
when you're making a movie,

00:08:05.155 --> 00:08:07.709
starts with the material

00:08:07.709 --> 00:08:10.236
and how well-written the script is.

00:08:10.236 --> 00:08:12.922
I've never seen a silk
purse made from a sow's ear.

00:08:12.922 --> 00:08:15.755
I've never seen it happen
wherein a crap script

00:08:15.755 --> 00:08:17.194
with crap characters,

00:08:17.194 --> 00:08:19.261
a director somehow found a way

00:08:19.261 --> 00:08:20.444
to make a masterpiece out of it.

00:08:20.444 --> 00:08:21.821
And that's the constant struggle

00:08:21.821 --> 00:08:24.410
that we all sort of have as actors,

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.140
is finding those gems.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:29.251
And that's why people grab
onto them like vultures.

00:08:29.251 --> 00:08:31.689
When you find a great piece
of material, or great script.

00:08:31.689 --> 00:08:33.906
It's like, you should see what happens.

00:08:33.906 --> 00:08:35.085
It's like pirana feeding fest.

00:08:35.085 --> 00:08:36.730
(host laughing)

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:37.754
Everyone wants it.

00:08:37.754 --> 00:08:39.341
Being an actor is the first thing

00:08:39.341 --> 00:08:40.922
that I remember wanting to do in life.

00:08:40.922 --> 00:08:43.255
I remember 14, 15-years-old,

00:08:44.522 --> 00:08:47.147
watching some classic films.

00:08:47.147 --> 00:08:49.594
Well, spending months and months watching

00:08:49.594 --> 00:08:50.874
all the great classics,

00:08:50.874 --> 00:08:53.562
all the great performers of the past

00:08:53.562 --> 00:08:56.459
and being so incredibly inspired by them

00:08:56.459 --> 00:08:57.608
and saying to myself,

00:08:57.608 --> 00:08:59.849
"One day, I want to come close
to doing something that good.

00:08:59.849 --> 00:09:02.856
So I think that thirst, or that drive,

00:09:02.856 --> 00:09:05.850
to just achieve what I believe
is something as good as

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.440
those heroes that I have

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.190
is what continues to
make me want to do this.

00:09:13.572 --> 00:09:16.667
- After This Boy's Life, you
went on to play Arnie Grape

00:09:16.667 --> 00:09:18.053
in What's Eating Gilbert Grape?

00:09:18.053 --> 00:09:20.188
And this was a turning point.

00:09:20.188 --> 00:09:24.355
I know. (laughs)
(audience applauding)

00:09:25.274 --> 00:09:28.159
This was a turning point in a lot of ways

00:09:28.159 --> 00:09:30.000
because I understand
you actually turned down

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.290
another movie just for the opportunity.

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:34.847
I don't even think you
had an audition lined up

00:09:34.847 --> 00:09:36.205
for Gilbert Grape.

00:09:36.205 --> 00:09:38.608
- I did have an audition
lined up for it, yeah.

00:09:38.608 --> 00:09:41.042
No, it was one of those situations where

00:09:41.042 --> 00:09:43.438
I did this movie, This Boy's Life,

00:09:43.438 --> 00:09:45.688
and it hadn't come out yet,

00:09:46.714 --> 00:09:48.409
but there was this one role

00:09:48.409 --> 00:09:50.104
that I wanted to play desperately,

00:09:50.104 --> 00:09:52.437
but I started to get offers,

00:09:53.883 --> 00:09:55.480
offers for other movies,

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:58.649
and there as a big Disney
movie they wanted me to do.

00:09:58.649 --> 00:10:01.202
And I don't know where
I got those little balls

00:10:01.202 --> 00:10:05.369
at 16-years-old to say, "No,
I'm going to wait it out

00:10:07.085 --> 00:10:09.118
"because I want to audition
for this other film."

00:10:09.118 --> 00:10:12.347
But, if there's one thing
that I'm really proud of

00:10:12.347 --> 00:10:15.147
in my entire career, it's that moment,

00:10:15.147 --> 00:10:17.306
at 16 to say that and do that

00:10:17.306 --> 00:10:21.716
and have that sort of conviction
of what I wanted to do.

00:10:21.716 --> 00:10:23.596
That's the one thing I'm really proud of.

00:10:23.596 --> 00:10:25.216
- 'Cause the other movie
was an offer, wasn't it?

00:10:25.216 --> 00:10:28.558
- Yeah, it was for a lot of
money (laughs) at that time,

00:10:28.558 --> 00:10:31.641
which sounded great at the time.

00:10:31.641 --> 00:10:33.593
But I said no, yeah.

00:10:33.593 --> 00:10:36.346
- So you've always been like a daredevil,

00:10:36.346 --> 00:10:40.174
but you've done, I think
I've talked to you about

00:10:40.174 --> 00:10:41.965
you swim with sharks, or you have.

00:10:41.965 --> 00:10:44.554
And you jump out of an airplane.

00:10:44.554 --> 00:10:46.698
On a regular basis or will you--

00:10:46.698 --> 00:10:47.558
- I only did that once.

00:10:47.558 --> 00:10:48.567
- Will you do it again?

00:10:48.567 --> 00:10:50.372
- When both parachutes don't open,

00:10:50.372 --> 00:10:52.585
you tend to not go repeat
something like that.

00:10:52.585 --> 00:10:54.900
- What are you talking about?

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:56.789
Well, then how'd you get down?
(audience laughing)

00:10:56.789 --> 00:10:57.873
- I had, (laughs)

00:10:57.873 --> 00:10:59.873
I jumped out of the airplane and then

00:10:59.873 --> 00:11:02.428
my first chute didn't open, they cut,

00:11:02.428 --> 00:11:04.246
it's tandem so somebody's on your back.

00:11:04.246 --> 00:11:05.275
They cut that line.

00:11:05.275 --> 00:11:08.041
We started free-falling towards earth

00:11:08.041 --> 00:11:10.688
and that's when you get
the 8 by 10 glossies

00:11:10.688 --> 00:11:11.521
of your whole life

00:11:11.521 --> 00:11:13.197
flashing before your eyes.
(audience laughing)

00:11:13.197 --> 00:11:15.820
And then the second one
was tangled as well.

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:18.377
And I saw all my friends
sort of popping off

00:11:18.377 --> 00:11:20.093
with their parachutes.

00:11:20.093 --> 00:11:22.708
And I'm still plummeting
towards planet Earth.

00:11:22.708 --> 00:11:27.003
And then that as tangled for
about a good, I don't know,

00:11:27.003 --> 00:11:28.188
20, 30, seconds.

00:11:28.188 --> 00:11:30.092
And then he untangled it.

00:11:30.092 --> 00:11:31.505
And then he told me,

00:11:31.505 --> 00:11:33.213
"Oh, you're probably going
to break your legs now

00:11:33.213 --> 00:11:34.988
"because we're going to too fast."

00:11:34.988 --> 00:11:36.922
So it was one of the worst
experiences of my life

00:11:36.922 --> 00:11:38.440
(audience laughing)
and I'll never do it again.

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:39.273
- And did you break your legs?

00:11:39.273 --> 00:11:40.284
- I did not break my legs.

00:11:40.284 --> 00:11:41.661
- Wow, so he's whispering in your ear,

00:11:41.661 --> 00:11:42.995
or yelling--
- He was screaming.

00:11:42.995 --> 00:11:45.701
(audience laughing)
- You're going to break

00:11:45.701 --> 00:11:47.258
your legs.
- You're going to break

00:11:47.258 --> 00:11:48.395
your legs.

00:11:48.395 --> 00:11:49.573
(audience laughing)
- What?

00:11:49.573 --> 00:11:50.990
I can't hear you!

00:11:52.051 --> 00:11:54.218
Wow, that's just horrible.

00:11:55.313 --> 00:11:57.368
So what's the scariest
thing you've ever done?

00:11:57.368 --> 00:12:01.844
- I was on a plane to Russia
and the engine exploded.

00:12:01.844 --> 00:12:05.493
I was looking out the
window and the entire engine

00:12:05.493 --> 00:12:06.563
just turned into a fireball.

00:12:06.563 --> 00:12:09.872
It was right after Sully had
that incident happen to him

00:12:09.872 --> 00:12:11.311
where the geese flew

00:12:11.311 --> 00:12:12.298
into both engines.
- Right, and he landed

00:12:12.298 --> 00:12:13.360
on the river.
- Yeah.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:14.893
This happened in one of the engines.

00:12:14.893 --> 00:12:19.153
And I was the only person
there that seemed to see this,

00:12:19.153 --> 00:12:20.567
but it was a flaming fireball.

00:12:20.567 --> 00:12:23.330
It was all Russian passengers.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:26.387
And I kind of felt like I'd already died

00:12:26.387 --> 00:12:28.256
and gone to heaven 'cause
no one said anything.

00:12:28.256 --> 00:12:30.113
(audience laughing)

00:12:30.113 --> 00:12:31.646
And I was screaming at the
top of my lungs saying,

00:12:31.646 --> 00:12:33.504
"What the hell is going one here?"

00:12:33.504 --> 00:12:35.422
And people just kind of looked back

00:12:35.422 --> 00:12:37.339
and the stewardess came out and said,

00:12:37.339 --> 00:12:39.593
"We seem to have a slight problem here."

00:12:39.593 --> 00:12:41.597
(audience laughing)

00:12:41.597 --> 00:12:44.861
And a Russian guy finally
said, "What is the problem?"

00:12:44.861 --> 00:12:46.221
(audience laughing)

00:12:46.221 --> 00:12:48.886
And they said, "Well, we
lost one of our engines."

00:12:48.886 --> 00:12:51.185
(audience laughing)

00:12:51.185 --> 00:12:54.134
And said, (laughs) he goes,
"How many engines did we have?"

00:12:54.134 --> 00:12:56.723
She goes, "Well, we had two

00:12:56.723 --> 00:13:00.369
"now we have one."
(audience laughing)

00:13:00.369 --> 00:13:03.952
And he proceeded to
say, "That is not good.

00:13:07.567 --> 00:13:09.007
"that is not good."
(audience laughing)

00:13:09.007 --> 00:13:11.187
And we basically dumped
fuel for 45 minutes

00:13:11.187 --> 00:13:15.102
and did an emergency landing
and all our tires exploded

00:13:15.102 --> 00:13:17.445
and there was 100
different ambulances there

00:13:17.445 --> 00:13:18.467
and it was on CNN.

00:13:18.467 --> 00:13:20.800
And that was another bummer.

00:13:23.385 --> 00:13:24.778
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:24.778 --> 00:13:28.203
I made this video because
Jacob Salem asked me to.

00:13:28.203 --> 00:13:29.931
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:29.931 --> 00:13:31.323
that you want me to profile next,

00:13:31.323 --> 00:13:33.006
leave it in the comments below

00:13:33.006 --> 00:13:34.334
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:34.334 --> 00:13:37.069
I'd also love to know which
of Leonardo's top 10 rules

00:13:37.069 --> 00:13:39.546
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:13:39.546 --> 00:13:41.390
What did you take from this video?

00:13:41.390 --> 00:13:43.111
Leave it in the comments below

00:13:43.111 --> 00:13:44.778
and I will join in the discussion.

00:13:44.778 --> 00:13:46.327
Thank you so much for watching.

00:13:46.327 --> 00:13:49.827
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

00:13:55.539 --> 00:13:59.332
- I always felt like outsider
because I knew of people

00:13:59.332 --> 00:14:01.506
that were in the industry,

00:14:01.506 --> 00:14:03.351
I just didn't know how to get into it.

00:14:03.351 --> 00:14:06.684
And I remember going to different agents

00:14:09.169 --> 00:14:11.092
when I was nine or 10-years-old.

00:14:11.092 --> 00:14:13.314
I was rejected twice.

00:14:13.314 --> 00:14:15.345
I remember there was like
this sort of cattle call

00:14:15.345 --> 00:14:17.212
of kids and I lined up.

00:14:17.212 --> 00:14:18.905
It was like a prison photo

00:14:18.905 --> 00:14:19.821
or something like that.
(audience laughing)

00:14:19.821 --> 00:14:21.084
And I'm standing there like this

00:14:21.084 --> 00:14:24.584
And I had this crazy break dancing haircut

00:14:26.183 --> 00:14:27.715
from when I was break dancing.

00:14:27.715 --> 00:14:29.991
And I kind of dressed in hip-hop clothing.

00:14:29.991 --> 00:14:32.366
And I remember they went, "Yes, yes."

00:14:32.366 --> 00:14:33.828
Went to me went, "Nope, yes, yes,

00:14:33.828 --> 00:14:35.827
"nope, yes, yes."
- Wow.

00:14:35.827 --> 00:14:37.711
- And that was it, so I got
rejected a couple times.

00:14:37.711 --> 00:14:40.439
And then I just kept asking
and asking and asking.

00:14:40.439 --> 00:14:42.632
I remember my dad saying,
"Just stick with it.

00:14:42.632 --> 00:14:44.083
"Some day you'll have your day."

00:14:44.083 --> 00:14:46.918
And then I came back,
I think two years later

00:14:46.918 --> 00:14:49.251
and finally got an agent.

00:14:49.251 --> 00:14:50.737
Meryl Streep and Diane Keaton

00:14:50.737 --> 00:14:53.489
were just my two favorite
actresses of all time,

00:14:53.489 --> 00:14:55.439
even before I did this film.

00:14:55.439 --> 00:14:59.606
And Meryl is just such presence
when you work with her.

00:15:00.675 --> 00:15:02.719
I mean, she throws you so
many curve balls all the time.

00:15:02.719 --> 00:15:05.133
You've got to be on your toes with her,

00:15:05.133 --> 00:15:07.985
because she may do something
completely different

00:15:07.985 --> 00:15:09.990
the next take, and you got
to sort of be prepared.

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:12.722
So anytime you work with
people of that caliber,

00:15:12.722 --> 00:15:14.215
you learn a lot.

00:15:14.215 --> 00:15:17.130
Making The Revenant was
about man's relationsihp

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:19.943
to the natural world, a world
that we collectively felt

00:15:19.943 --> 00:15:23.914
in 2015 as the hottest
year in recorded history.

00:15:23.914 --> 00:15:26.791
Our production needed to
move to the southern tip

00:15:26.791 --> 00:15:28.358
of this planet just to
be able to find snow.

00:15:28.358 --> 00:15:30.276
Climate change is real.

00:15:30.276 --> 00:15:32.099
It is happening right now.

00:15:32.099 --> 00:15:36.935
It is the most urgent threat
facing our entire species

00:15:36.935 --> 00:15:40.307
and we need to work collectively together

00:15:40.307 --> 00:15:42.390
and stop procrastinating.

00:15:43.612 --> 00:15:46.136
We need to support
leaders around the world

00:15:46.136 --> 00:15:49.224
who do not speak for the big polluters

00:15:49.224 --> 00:15:52.475
or the big corporations, but
who speak for all of humanity,

00:15:52.475 --> 00:15:54.796
for the indigenous people of the world,

00:15:54.796 --> 00:15:57.920
for the billions and billions
of underprivileged people

00:15:57.920 --> 00:15:59.638
who will be most affected by this,

00:15:59.638 --> 00:16:01.681
for our children's children,

00:16:01.681 --> 00:16:04.328
and for those people
out there whose voices

00:16:04.328 --> 00:16:06.587
have been drowned out by
the politics of greed.

00:16:06.587 --> 00:16:09.623
I thank you all for this
amazing award tonight.

00:16:09.623 --> 00:16:11.991
Let us not take this planet for granted.

00:16:11.991 --> 00:16:14.670
I do not take tonight for granted.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:15.675
Thank you so very much.

